</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.2.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">
<span id="S6.T1.2.9.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="S6.T1.2.9.4.1.1" class="ltx_p">5.768 <math id="S6.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.273</span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Average and worst-case errors, with standard deviation, for the naive and robust utility reconstruction procedures, both averaged over 100 Monte-Carlo simulations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table I ‣ VI-D Robust Utility Estimation ‣ VI Numerical Examples. Detection of Pareto-Efficient Coordination in Multi-UAV Systems ‣ Multi-Agent Inverse RL for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination – Distributionally Robust Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> displays the average error and worst-case error, averaged over 100 Monte-Carlo simulations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Observe that while Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ IV-E 𝛿-Optimal Robust Utility Reconstruction via Finite Reduction ‣ IV Main Result II. Distributionally Robust Utility Reconstruction ‣ Multi-Agent Inverse RL for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination – Distributionally Robust Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> performs similarly to the naive reconstruction on average, its performance is significantly improved in the worst-case. Thus, we verify that Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ IV-E 𝛿-Optimal Robust Utility Reconstruction via Finite Reduction ‣ IV Main Result II. Distributionally Robust Utility Reconstruction ‣ Multi-Agent Inverse RL for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination – Distributionally Robust Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> achieves <span id="S6.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">distributionally robust</span> utility estimation, <span id="S6.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">without sacrificing average performance</span>. The distributional robustness is apparent from the reduced worst-case error, and serves as the advantage of this approach.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08956v1/AlgConvg3.png" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/163;" width="476" height="163" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span> Convergence of <math id="S6.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CV</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ IV-E 𝛿-Optimal Robust Utility Reconstruction via Finite Reduction ‣ IV Main Result II. Distributionally Robust Utility Reconstruction ‣ Multi-Agent Inverse RL for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination – Distributionally Robust Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, with respect to varying Wasserstein-radii <math id="S6.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ IV-E 𝛿-Optimal Robust Utility Reconstruction via Finite Reduction ‣ IV Main Result II. Distributionally Robust Utility Reconstruction ‣ Multi-Agent Inverse RL for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination – Distributionally Robust Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> terminates when <math id="S6.F6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CV&lt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CV&lt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and produces a <math id="S6.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal solution of (<a href="#S4.E23" title="Equation 23 ‣ IV-C3 Wasserstein Distributionally Robust Utility Estimation ‣ IV-C Utility Reconstruction: Distributionally Robust (DRO) Approach to Utility Reconstruction ‣ IV Main Result II. Distributionally Robust Utility Reconstruction ‣ Multi-Agent Inverse RL for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination – Distributionally Robust Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>). It can be seen that Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ IV-E 𝛿-Optimal Robust Utility Reconstruction via Finite Reduction ‣ IV Main Result II. Distributionally Robust Utility Reconstruction ‣ Multi-Agent Inverse RL for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination – Distributionally Robust Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> produces a <math id="S6.F6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal solution within 10 iterations for each Wasserstein proximity <math id="S6.F6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the apparent complexity of the semi-infinite optimization (<a href="#S4.E24" title="Equation 24 ‣ Theorem 3 (Semi-Infinite Reformulation). ‣ IV-D Semi-Infinite Programming Approach to Solve the DRO ‣ IV Main Result II. Distributionally Robust Utility Reconstruction ‣ Multi-Agent Inverse RL for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination – Distributionally Robust Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>), Figure <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ VI-D Robust Utility Estimation ‣ VI Numerical Examples. Detection of Pareto-Efficient Coordination in Multi-UAV Systems ‣ Multi-Agent Inverse RL for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination – Distributionally Robust Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows that a <math id="S6.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal solution from Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ IV-E 𝛿-Optimal Robust Utility Reconstruction via Finite Reduction ‣ IV Main Result II. Distributionally Robust Utility Reconstruction ‣ Multi-Agent Inverse RL for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination – Distributionally Robust Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> can be achieved rapidly. Each curve is the average of 100 Monte-Carlo simulations, for different Wasserstein radii <math id="S6.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. In each case Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ IV-E 𝛿-Optimal Robust Utility Reconstruction via Finite Reduction ‣ IV Main Result II. Distributionally Robust Utility Reconstruction ‣ Multi-Agent Inverse RL for Identifying Pareto-Efficient Coordination – Distributionally Robust Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> produces a <math id="S6.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal solution on average within 10 iterations for <math id="S6.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have developed a principled framework for distributionally robust multi-agent inverse reinforcement learning, that is, detecting coordination and reconstructing
utilities in multi-agent systems. By abstracting system dynamics into a
linearly constrained multi-objective optimization, we established necessary and
sufficient conditions for a dataset of observed behavior to be consistent
with Pareto-efficient coordination. This equivalence to a linear program provides a
tractable test for coordination and, when satisfied, enables the explicit reconstruction
of utility functions that rationalize the observed behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To handle noisy measurement regimes, we derived an optimal statistical detector with
provable guarantees on Type-I error, and introduced a distributionally robust utility
reconstruction procedure that substantially reduces worst-case error while preserving
average accuracy. We provided an extended example demonstrating how these mathematical formulations are naturally derived from
multi-UAV covert coordination. Numerical results demonstrated the
effectiveness of both the detector and the robust reconstruction scheme.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although illustrated in the context of multi-UAV coordination, the methodology is more general. It applies
to any multi-agent system where heterogeneous objectives are optimized under shared
linear constraints, including wireless communication networks, vehicle platoons, smart
grids, and cooperative robotics. In such settings, our framework allows an analyst
to rigorously test for coordination, robustly recover the hidden objectives that drive system
behavior, and thus anticipate responses to future environmental constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
